After experimenting with connection and failure recovery, I've found the current backoff strategy slightly aggressive for certain applications. Under a total service failure with extended downtime, recovery can take several minutes waiting for backoff to retry and come back up. This is even more problematic when a running service may have several instances coming up in a random order, causing more delay under failure.

Rather than bikeshed the backoff algorithm, it seems most reasonable to provide a client option, `WithBackoff`, that would let one configure the delay time as a function of the number of retries. The options full signature would be as follows:

``` go
func WithBackoff(strategy BackoffStrategy) DialOption
```

We'd also define the type `BackoffStrategy`, with the following signature:

``` go
type BackoffStrategy interface {
    Backoff(retries int) time.Duration
}
```

A base implementation, exposing configuration of backoff parameters, could be as follows, cribbed from https://github.com/grpc/grpc-go/blob/master/rpc_util.go#L414:

``` go
type BackoffConfig {
    BaseDelay time.Duration
    MaxDelay time.Duration
    Factor float64
    Jitter float64
}

func (bc *BackconfConfig) Backoff(retries int) time.Duration { /*...*/ }

var DefaultBackoff = BackoffConfig{
    BaseDelay: time.Second,
    MaxDelay: 120 *time.Second,
    Factor: 1.6,
    Jitter: 0.2,
}

opt := WithBackoff(DefaultBackoff)
```

Another use case for this would be a schedule reconnect time for deterministic, planned downtime, which might look like this:

``` go
type ScheduledBackoff struct {
    // after sets the time when connections should be retried.
    after time.Time
    mu sync.Mutex
}

func (sb *ScheduledBackoff) Set(after time.Time) {
    sb.mu.Lock()
    defer sb.mu.Unlock()
    sb.after = after
}

func (sb *ScheduledBackoff) Backoff(retries int) time.Duration {
    sb.mu.Lock()
    defer sb.mu.Unlock()

    wait := time.after.Sub(time.Now())
    if wait < 0 {
        return 0
    }

    return wait
}
```

In this example, we have an application defined backoff strategy that could be set elsewhere to allow reconnection after a certain time. Obviously the above isn't complete for a production implementation, but I hope you could see the utility. This could also be used to implemented circuit breakers or other strategies controlling reconnection of services.

If this sounds like a reasonable proposal, I can submit a PR with the core functionality.
